JamesDSP (rootless) -- replaces AudioFX in this ROM.

The APK is NOT committed here. Run ../../fetch-jamesdsp.sh to fetch and verify
it; that script also writes the Android.bp and privapp-permissions XML next to
the APK. Until it is run this directory holds only this README, so the tree
still builds and AudioFX stays in place.

Pinned artifact
    me.timschneeberger.rootlessjamesdsp  1.6.14 (versionCode 51)
    https://f-droid.org/repo/me.timschneeberger.rootlessjamesdsp_51.apk
    sha256 4cdd6cbc57abb00b1ce28668dc29c021640bae8c4521ec592efc58c4239d83a1
    38,647,450 bytes, minSdk 29, targetSdk 34, arm64-v8a + armeabi-v7a

Why F-Droid and not GitHub: the upstream GitHub releases carry no APK assets at
all -- the project publishes through F-Droid, which builds from source and
serves a stable, checksummable URL per versionCode.

WHAT THIS CHANGES, AND THE HONEST TRADE-OFF
    AudioFX drives the VENDOR audio effects (equalizer, bass boost, virtualizer)
    through the platform AudioEffect API. JamesDSP rootless does not: it captures
    playback with the AudioPlaybackCapture API, processes it, and re-renders.

    Consequences, none of which are bugs:
      - apps that set allowAudioPlaybackCapture="false" (most DRM video, some
        streaming apps) CANNOT be processed, by Android design
      - a small amount of added latency
      - a persistent foreground-service notification

    It is a far more capable DSP than AudioFX in exchange for those. If the
    vendor effect chain matters more than the DSP, keep AudioFX and skip this.

WHY PRIVILEGED, AND WHAT IT COSTS
    Without android.permission.DUMP the app cannot see audio sessions on its own,
    so the user must re-accept a MediaProjection capture dialog after EVERY
    reboot. That reads as a broken feature in a shipped ROM.

    The generated XML allowlists exactly one permission, DUMP, for exactly this
    package. DUMP allows reading system service state (dumpsys). That is a real
    privilege for a bundled third-party app -- it is FOSS and reproducibly built
    by F-Droid, which is why it is defensible here, but it IS a decision. To opt
    out, delete the privapp XML and drop privileged:true from the Android.bp; the
    app still works with the per-boot dialog.
